#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179465 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179626 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c6535bf-7dd1-5231-a992-9aefb8066499" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations With Unknown Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "ddcd149b-a90b-5ed2-923d-75e8a84ff145" :question/math-academy-id "e-13901" :question/problem "Solve the equation $5ky-13=7$ for the variable $y$." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $y$. To do this, we first add $13$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5ky - 13 &= 7 \\\\\n5ky - 13 + 13 &= 7 + 13 \\\\\n5ky &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $5k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5ky &= 20 \\\\\n\\frac{5ky}{5k} &= \\frac{20}{5k} \\\\\n\\frac{5ky}{5k} &= \\frac{20}{5k} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{20}{5k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{5 \\cdot 4}{5k} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{5 \\cdot 4}{5k} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{4}{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179627 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aefe9086-38a5-5055-9f95-56eafacf518b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Containing Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "6eb1b4ba-3182-5ecc-8ae0-72ec27036b29" :question/math-academy-id "e-13902" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 34 kx + 9 = 2$ for the variable $x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{4}kx + 9 &= 2 \\\\\n4 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}kx + 9) &= 4 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n3kx + 36 &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to isolate $x$. To do this, we first subtract $36$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3kx + 36 &= 8 \\\\\n3kx + 36 - 36 &= 8 - 36 \\\\\n3kx &= -28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $3k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3kx &= -28 \\\\\n\\frac{3kx}{3k} &= \\frac{-28}{3k} \\\\\n\\frac{3kx}{3k} &= \\frac{-28}{3k} \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{28}{3k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179468 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41be5b01-b9ea-576b-a7e5-d522b8b1dc88" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Using Cross Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "b35596e5-5914-502f-87dd-89348ab1664c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13904" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{1}{3} = -\\dfrac{ax}{5}$ for the variable $x$." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $x$. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3} &= -\\frac{ax}{5} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3} &= \\frac{-ax}{5} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 1 &= 3 \\cdot (-ax) \\\\\n5 &= -3ax\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $-3a$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 &= -3ax \\\\\n\\frac{5}{-3a} &= \\frac{-3ax}{-3a} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{-3a} &= \\frac{-3ax}{-3a} \\\\\n- \\frac{5}{3a} &= x \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{5}{3a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
